{"track":"tahsili","num":"6.01","slug":"limits-derivatives","title":"Limits & Derivatives","subtitle":"ClipSAT Printable Practice Suite (clipsat.org) • Topic 6.01 • 20 FRQs + 25 MCQs • Difficulty Progression • Cross-Lesson Synthesis","sections":[{"heading":"Part I — Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","questions":[{"q":"The graph of $f(x)=x^2-2x$ is shown. Find the equation of the tangent line at $x=3$ (the marked point).","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Tangent lines","answer":"$f(3)=9-6=3$. $f'(x)=2x-2$, so $f'(3)=4$. $y-3=4(x-3)\\Rightarrow y=4x-9$.","explanation":"$f(3)=9-6=3$. $f'(x)=2x-2$, so $f'(3)=4$. $y-3=4(x-3)\\Rightarrow y=4x-9$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["x**2-2*x"],"xmin":-1,"xmax":5,"ymin":-2,"ymax":10,"points":[[3,3]],"width":220}},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow2}(3x^2-4x+1)$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","answer":"$3(4)-8+1=12-8+1=5$.","explanation":"$3(4)-8+1=12-8+1=5$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["1 + 1.5/(x - (-1))"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"asymptotes":{"x":[-1],"y":[1]},"point_labels":[{"x":0.5,"y":2,"label":"$( 0.5, 2 )$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow3}\\frac{x^2-9}{x-3}$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","answer":"$\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}=x+3\\rightarrow6$.","explanation":"$\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}=x+3\\rightarrow6$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["1 + 1.5/(x - (-1))"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"asymptotes":{"x":[-1],"y":[1]},"point_labels":[{"x":0.5,"y":2,"label":"$( 0.5, 2 )$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow\\infty}\\frac{3x^2+2x}{x^2-5}$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Intermediate Multi-Step Application (8–14)","answer":"The degrees match, so the limit is the ratio of leading coefficients: $3$.","explanation":"The degrees match, so the limit is the ratio of leading coefficients: $3$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["1 + 1.5/(x - (-1))"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"asymptotes":{"x":[-1],"y":[1]},"point_labels":[{"x":0.5,"y":2,"label":"$( 0.5, 2 )$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow2}(3x^2-4x+1)$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Evaluating limits by direct substitution","answer":"$3(4)-8+1=12-8+1=5$.","explanation":"$3(4)-8+1=12-8+1=5$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow-1}(x^3+2x-5)$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Evaluating limits by direct substitution","answer":"$(-1)^3+2(-1)-5=-1-2-5=-8$.","explanation":"$(-1)^3+2(-1)-5=-1-2-5=-8$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["1 + 1.5/(x - (-1))"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"asymptotes":{"x":[-1],"y":[1]},"point_labels":[{"x":0.5,"y":2,"label":"$( 0.5, 2 )$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow0}\\frac{5x+7}{x+3}$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Evaluating limits by direct substitution","answer":"$\\frac{5(0)+7}{0+3}=\\frac{7}{3}$.","explanation":"$\\frac{5(0)+7}{0+3}=\\frac{7}{3}$."}]},{"heading":"Part II — Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Intermediate Multi-Step Application (8–14)","questions":[{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow3}\\sqrt{x+13}$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Evaluating limits by direct substitution","answer":"$\\sqrt{3+13}=\\sqrt{16}=4$.","explanation":"$\\sqrt{3+13}=\\sqrt{16}=4$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow3}\\frac{x^2-9}{x-3}$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Evaluating limits by factoring (the 0/0 form)","answer":"$\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}=x+3\\rightarrow6$.","explanation":"$\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}=x+3\\rightarrow6$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow-2}\\frac{x^2+5x+6}{x+2}$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Evaluating limits by factoring (the 0/0 form)","answer":"$\\frac{(x+2)(x+3)}{x+2}=x+3\\rightarrow1$.","explanation":"$\\frac{(x+2)(x+3)}{x+2}=x+3\\rightarrow1$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["1 + 1.5/(x - (-1))"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"asymptotes":{"x":[-1],"y":[1]},"point_labels":[{"x":0.5,"y":2,"label":"$( 0.5, 2 )$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow4}\\frac{x^2-16}{x-4}$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Evaluating limits by factoring (the 0/0 form)","answer":"$\\frac{(x-4)(x+4)}{x-4}=x+4\\rightarrow8$.","explanation":"$\\frac{(x-4)(x+4)}{x-4}=x+4\\rightarrow8$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow1}\\frac{x^2-1}{x^2-3x+2}$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Evaluating limits by factoring (the 0/0 form)","answer":"$\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x-2)}=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}\\rightarrow\\frac{2}{-1}=-2$.","explanation":"$\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x-2)}=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}\\rightarrow\\frac{2}{-1}=-2$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow\\infty}\\frac{3x^2+2x}{x^2-5}$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Limits at infinity","answer":"The degrees match, so the limit is the ratio of leading coefficients: $3$.","explanation":"The degrees match, so the limit is the ratio of leading coefficients: $3$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow\\infty}\\frac{2x+7}{x^2+1}$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Limits at infinity","answer":"The denominator has higher degree, so the limit is $0$.","explanation":"The denominator has higher degree, so the limit is $0$."}]},{"heading":"Part III — Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (15–20)","questions":[{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow\\infty}\\frac{5x^3-2}{x^3+4x}$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"Limits at infinity","cross_lesson":"2.01 Functions & Transformations","answer":"The degrees match, so the limit is the ratio of leading coefficients: $5$.","explanation":"The degrees match, so the limit is the ratio of leading coefficients: $5$."},{"q":"A function is defined by $f(x)=x+1$ for $x<2$, and $f(x)=2x-1$ for $x\\geq2$. Find $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow2^-}f(x)$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"One-sided limits","cross_lesson":"3.01 Sequences & Series","answer":"Using the first rule: $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow2^-}f(x)=2+1=3$.","explanation":"Using the first rule: $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow2^-}f(x)=2+1=3$."},{"q":"Using the same piecewise function, find $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow2^+}f(x)$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"One-sided limits","cross_lesson":"4.01 Trigonometry","answer":"Using the second rule: $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow2^+}f(x)=2(2)-1=3$.","explanation":"Using the second rule: $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow2^+}f(x)=2(2)-1=3$."},{"q":"Using the same piecewise function, does $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow2}f(x)$ exist? State its value.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"One-sided limits","cross_lesson":"5.01 Geometry & Coordinate Geometry","answer":"Yes — both one-sided limits equal $3$, so $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow2}f(x)=3$.","explanation":"Yes — both one-sided limits equal $3$, so $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow2}f(x)=3$."},{"q":"Differentiate $f(x)=x^5$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"The power rule","cross_lesson":"7.01 Integration & Applications","answer":"$f'(x)=5x^4$.","explanation":"$f'(x)=5x^4$."},{"q":"Differentiate $f(x)=3x^4-2x^2+7$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"FRQ","target":"The power rule","cross_lesson":"8.01 Exponentials & Logarithms","answer":"$f'(x)=12x^3-4x$.","explanation":"$f'(x)=12x^3-4x$."}]},{"heading":"Part IV — Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","questions":[{"q":"The graph of $f(x)=x^2-2x$ is shown, with the point at $x=3$ marked. Find the equation of the tangent line there.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — Tangent lines","choices":["$y=3x-9$","$y=4x-3$","$y=4x+9$","$y=4x-9$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$f(3)=3$, $f'(3)=4$: $y-3=4(x-3)\\Rightarrow y=4x-9$.","explanation":"$f(3)=3$, $f'(3)=4$: $y-3=4(x-3)\\Rightarrow y=4x-9$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["x**2-2*x"],"xmin":-1,"xmax":5,"ymin":-2,"ymax":10,"points":[[3,3]],"width":230}},{"q":"The graph shows $f(x)=x^2$ and its tangent line at $x=2$. Find the slope of the tangent line by reading the graph.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — Tangent lines","choices":["$4$","$2$","$1$","$8$"],"correct":0,"answer":"The tangent line rises $4$ for every $1$ across.","explanation":"The tangent line rises $4$ for every $1$ across.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["x**2","4*x-4"],"xmin":0,"xmax":4,"ymin":-1,"ymax":10,"width":230}},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow3}(2x^2-5x+3)$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","choices":["$12$","$-6$","$18$","$6$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$2(9)-5(3)+3=18-15+3=6$.","explanation":"$2(9)-5(3)+3=18-15+3=6$.","figure":{"type":"plot","fns":["2 + 1.5/(x - (1))"],"xmin":-5,"xmax":5,"ymin":-4,"ymax":6,"asymptotes":{"x":[1],"y":[2]},"point_labels":[{"x":2.5,"y":3,"label":"$( 2.5, 3 )$"}],"width":230}},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow3}(2x^2-5x+3)$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — Evaluating limits by direct substitution","choices":["$12$","$-6$","$18$","$6$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$2(9)-5(3)+3=18-15+3=6$.","explanation":"$2(9)-5(3)+3=18-15+3=6$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow4}\\frac{x^2-3x}{2x+1}$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — Evaluating limits by direct substitution","choices":["$\\frac{4}{7}$","$\\frac{16}{9}$","$\\frac{4}{8}$","$\\frac{4}{9}$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$\\frac{16-12}{9}=\\frac{4}{9}$.","explanation":"$\\frac{16-12}{9}=\\frac{4}{9}$."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow\\infty}\\frac{4x^2+3x}{2x^2-7}$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — Limits at infinity","choices":["$2$","$0$","$\\frac{3}{7}$","$4$"],"correct":0,"answer":"Same degree: ratio of leading coefficients $=\\frac{4}{2}=2$.","explanation":"Same degree: ratio of leading coefficients $=\\frac{4}{2}=2$."},{"q":"A function is defined by $f(x)=x+2$ for $x<3$, and $f(x)=3x-4$ for $x\\geq3$. Find $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow3^-}f(x)$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — One-sided limits","choices":["$3$","$4$","$9$","$5$"],"correct":3,"answer":"Using $x<3$: $f(x)\\rightarrow3+2=5$.","explanation":"Using $x<3$: $f(x)\\rightarrow3+2=5$."},{"q":"Differentiate $f(x)=x^7$.","difficulty":"Foundational","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — The power rule","choices":["$x^6$","$7x^8$","$6x^7$","$7x^6$"],"correct":3,"answer":"Power rule: $7x^{7-1}=7x^6$.","explanation":"Power rule: $7x^{7-1}=7x^6$."}]},{"heading":"Part V — Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","questions":[{"q":"Differentiate $f(x)=x^3(2x+5)$ using the product rule.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — The product rule","choices":["$8x^3+15x^2$","$6x^2+5$","$8x^3+5x^2$","$2x^3+15x^2$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$f'(x)=3x^2(2x+5)+x^3(2)=6x^3+15x^2+2x^3=8x^3+15x^2$.","explanation":"$f'(x)=3x^2(2x+5)+x^3(2)=6x^3+15x^2+2x^3=8x^3+15x^2$."},{"q":"Differentiate $f(x)=(2x+3)^5$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — The chain rule","choices":["$5(2x+3)^4$","$2(2x+3)^4$","$10(2x+3)^5$","$10(2x+3)^4$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$f'(x)=5(2x+3)^4\\cdot2=10(2x+3)^4$.","explanation":"$f'(x)=5(2x+3)^4\\cdot2=10(2x+3)^4$."},{"q":"Differentiate $f(x)=(x^3+2)^2$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — The chain rule","choices":["$6x^2(x^3+2)$","$6x^2(x^3+2)^2$","$3x^2(x^3+2)$","$2(x^3+2)$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$f'(x)=2(x^3+2)\\cdot3x^2=6x^2(x^3+2)$.","explanation":"$f'(x)=2(x^3+2)\\cdot3x^2=6x^2(x^3+2)$."},{"q":"Find the slope of the tangent line to $f(x)=x^2$ at $x=4$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — Tangent lines","choices":["$4$","$2$","$16$","$8$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$f'(x)=2x$; $f'(4)=8$.","explanation":"$f'(x)=2x$; $f'(4)=8$."},{"q":"For $f(x)=-x^2+8x$, determine the interval where $f$ is decreasing.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — Increasing, decreasing, and the second derivative","choices":["$x<4$","$x>8$","$x>4$","$x<8$"],"correct":2,"answer":"$f'(x)=-2x+8<0\\Rightarrow x>4$.","explanation":"$f'(x)=-2x+8<0\\Rightarrow x>4$."},{"q":"The height of a ball thrown upward is given by $h(t)=-5t^2+30t+3$ (in meters). Find the time at which the ball reaches its maximum height.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — Word problems: rates of change","choices":["$t=3$","$t=5$","$t=6$","$t=2$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$h'(t)=-10t+30=0\\Rightarrow t=3$ seconds.","explanation":"$h'(t)=-10t+30=0\\Rightarrow t=3$ seconds."},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow-1}(2x^2+8x-9)$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — One-sided limits","choices":["$-18$","$15$","$-12$","$-15$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$2(-1)^2-8-9=-15$.","explanation":"$2(-1)^2-8-9=-15$."},{"q":"Differentiate $f(x)=x^4-2x$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — The power rule","choices":["$5x^3-2$","$4x^3-2$","$4x^4-2$","$x^4-2$"],"correct":1,"answer":"$f'(x)=4x^3-2$.","explanation":"$f'(x)=4x^3-2$."},{"q":"Differentiate $f(x)=3\\cos x$.","difficulty":"Intermediate","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — The product rule","choices":["$-3\\sin x$","$3\\cos x$","$3\\sin x$","$4-\\sin x$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$f'(x)=-3\\sin x$.","explanation":"$f'(x)=-3\\sin x$."}]},{"heading":"Part VI — Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Challenging & Cross-Lesson Synthesis (38–45)","questions":[{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow-1}\\frac{x^2-2x-3}{x+1}$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — The chain rule","choices":["$4$","$-4$","$-6$","$-2$"],"correct":1,"answer":"$\\frac{(x+1)(x-3)}{x+1}=x-3\\rightarrow-4$.","explanation":"$\\frac{(x+1)(x-3)}{x+1}=x-3\\rightarrow-4$.","cross_lesson":"5.01 Geometry & Coordinate Geometry"},{"q":"Differentiate $f(x)=(3x+6)(-2x-6)$ using the product rule.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — Derivatives of trigonometric and exponential functions","choices":["$-12x-30$","$-12x+30$","$-12x-28$","$12x-30$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$f'(x)=(3)(-2x-6)+(3x+6)(-2)=-6x-18-6x-12=-12x-30$.","explanation":"$f'(x)=(3)(-2x-6)+(3x+6)(-2)=-6x-18-6x-12=-12x-30$.","cross_lesson":"7.01 Integration & Applications"},{"q":"Differentiate $f(x)=(-3x-4)^{5}$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — Tangent lines","choices":["$-18(-3x-4)^4$","$-15(-3x-4)^5$","$-15(-3x-4)^4$","$5(-3x-4)^4$"],"correct":2,"answer":"$f'(x)=5(-3x-4)^{4}(-3)=-15(-3x-4)^4$.","explanation":"$f'(x)=5(-3x-4)^{4}(-3)=-15(-3x-4)^4$.","cross_lesson":"8.01 Exponentials & Logarithms"},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow\\infty}\\frac{4x^2+8x}{3x^2-8}$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — Increasing, decreasing, and the second derivative","choices":["$0$","$\\frac{7}{3}$","$\\frac{3}{4}$","$\\frac{4}{3}$"],"correct":3,"answer":"The degrees match, so the limit is the ratio of leading coefficients: $\\frac{4}{3}$.","explanation":"The degrees match, so the limit is the ratio of leading coefficients: $\\frac{4}{3}$.","cross_lesson":"9.01 Statistics & Probability"},{"q":"Find the equation of the tangent line to $f(x)=2x^2+3x-1$ at $x=-3$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"MCQ — Word problems: rates of change","choices":["$y=9x-19$","$y=-9x+19$","$y=-9x-16$","$y=-9x-19$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$f(-3)=8$, $f'(-3)=-9$. $y-8=-9(x-(-3))\\Rightarrow y=-9x-19$.","explanation":"$f(-3)=8$, $f'(-3)=-9$. $y-8=-9(x-(-3))\\Rightarrow y=-9x-19$.","cross_lesson":"1.01 Algebra & Equations"},{"q":"For $f(x)=-x^2-3$, determine the interval where $f$ is increasing.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Free-Response Questions (FRQs): Foundational Fluency (1–7)","choices":["$x<0$","$x>2$","$x<2$","$x>0$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$f'(x)=-2x$. Increasing when $f'(x)>0$, i.e. $x<0$.","explanation":"$f'(x)=-2x$. Increasing when $f'(x)>0$, i.e. $x<0$.","cross_lesson":"2.01 Functions & Transformations"},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow4}\\frac{x^2-3x}{2x+1}$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Foundational Concept Mastery (21–28)","choices":["$\\frac{4}{7}$","$\\frac{16}{9}$","$\\frac{4}{8}$","$\\frac{4}{9}$"],"correct":3,"answer":"$\\frac{16-12}{9}=\\frac{4}{9}$.","explanation":"$\\frac{16-12}{9}=\\frac{4}{9}$.","cross_lesson":"3.01 Sequences & Series"},{"q":"Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow5}\\frac{x^2+x-30}{x-5}$.","difficulty":"Challenging","question_type":"MCQ","target":"Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQs): Intermediate Exam Application (29–37)","choices":["$11$","$13$","$-11$","$9$"],"correct":0,"answer":"$\\frac{(x-5)(x+6)}{x-5}=x+6\\rightarrow11$.","explanation":"$\\frac{(x-5)(x+6)}{x-5}=x+6\\rightarrow11$.","cross_lesson":"4.01 Trigonometry"}]}]}